============
Vector operations in each work item IMO should be split up into `n' instructions for computation. In that regard there is no value in 
vectorization.

Though vector load/stores will gain from burst load/stores. So memory accesses do gain from vectorization

Data Dependency can be hidden by having a wavefront be unrolled in as many cycles as the max length of the Pipeline. That assumes the 2 instructions 
use the same register at the same vector mapping.
Also the compiler can always issue unrelated instructions in the shadow or worst case insert wait states

For memory loads one would have to introduce S_WAITCNT like instructions to explicitly wait since these are not fixed #cycles
https://www.nvidia.com/content/PDF/kepler/NVIDIA-Kepler-GK110-Architecture-Whitepaper.pdf
Read the second paragraph of "Quad Warp Scheduler" Essentially you need Dependency tracking for Texture sampler / Memory Loads and you need them for 
MATH Units to avoid RAW. The latter is fixed #cycles and can be hidden by 
* compiler inserted wait states
* inserting independent instructions (in the shadow of the wait)
* Warp unrolling can take as many cycles as the worst case math pipeline depth

